---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/docs/Callout.astro';
import Table from '../../components/docs/Table.astro';
import Code from '../../components/docs/Code.astro';
import { page } from '../../data/docs-nav';
import { EXPORT_FORMATS, DELIVERY_FORMATS } from '../../data/docs';

const entry = page('/docs/formats/');

const picker = EXPORT_FORMATS.filter((format) => format.inPicker);
const engineOnly = EXPORT_FORMATS.filter((format) => !format.inPicker);

const sqliteQuery = `-- Everything that happened last Tuesday, across every type:
SELECT local_day, type, value, unit, source_name
FROM record
WHERE local_day = '2026-08-18'
ORDER BY start_date;

-- Anything the columns left out is still there:
SELECT json_extract(raw, '$.metadata.HKTimeZone.value') AS tz
FROM sample
WHERE type = 'HKQuantityTypeIdentifierStepCount'
LIMIT 5;`;

const frontMatter = `---
date: 2026-08-18
steps: 8241
sleep_hours: 7.2
workout_minutes: 42
resting_heart_rate: 54
---`;
---

<DocsLayout page={entry}>
  <p>
    There are two different lists here, because there are two different things Hozz does. A{' '}
    <strong>manual export</strong> produces a file you save or share from the phone. An{' '}
    <strong>automatic destination</strong> receives batches of new records as they arrive. They do
    not offer the same formats, and the reasons are worth knowing.
  </p>

  <h2>Manual export formats</h2>

  <Table
    caption="Manual export formats, what each is good for, and what each keeps"
    columns={['Format', 'File', 'Good for', 'Keeps']}
  >
    {
      picker.map((format) => (
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">
            {format.name}
            {format.lossy && <> — lossy</>}
          </th>
          <td>
            <code>.{format.fileExtension}</code>
          </td>
          <td>{format.good}</td>
          <td>{format.keeps}</td>
        </tr>
      ))
    }
  </Table>

  {
    engineOnly.map((format) => (
      <p>
        <strong>{format.name}</strong> ({format.keeps}) {format.good.toLowerCase()}
      </p>
    ))
  }

  <Callout kind="lossy" title="Three of these deliberately throw detail away">
    <p>
      <strong>CSV</strong> and <strong>Markdown</strong> are lossy projections: they take everything
      in the export and keep less of each record. <strong>GPX</strong> is lossy in a different and
      more surprising way — it takes almost nothing in the export and keeps all of what it takes.
    </p>
    <p>
      All three say so in the app, at the moment you choose them, rather than only here. If you want
      an archive, use NDJSON or SQLite.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h3>NDJSON — the default</h3>

  <p>
    One record per line, streamable at any size, and lossless for every field Hozz encodes. It is
    also the format the export is internally built from, so producing it costs nothing extra: the
    parts are copied verbatim rather than read and rewritten.
  </p>

  <h3>SQLite — for asking questions</h3>

  <p>
    Query it in Datasette, DuckDB, pandas, Grafana or <code>sqlite3</code> with no import step. The
    schema is built for asking questions rather than for mirroring the JSON: everything time-shaped
    except workouts goes into one wide <code>sample</code> table with a <code>type</code> column,
    because the queries worth having are cross-type, and a table per type turns those into a
    hundred-way union.
  </p>

  <p>
    Workouts, deletions and characteristics have genuinely different shapes and get their own
    tables. A <code>record</code> view puts the time-shaped ones back on one timeline, and a{' '}
    <code>daily</code> view covers the aggregate most questions start from.
  </p>

  <Code caption="Querying an export" code={sqliteQuery} />

  <p>
    <strong>SQLite is not marked lossy</strong>, and that is a real distinction rather than a
    generous one: every row also stores the untouched export line in <code>raw</code>, so anything
    the typed columns leave out is still reachable with <code>json_extract</code>. A{' '}
    <code>meta</code> table records when the export ran, what it covered, and which time zone the{' '}
    <code>local_day</code> columns were bucketed in — a UTC day would file an evening workout under
    the following morning.
  </p>

  <h3>CSV — for spreadsheets</h3>

  <p>
    One CSV per Health type, plus deletion and export-log files when needed. Opens in Excel, Numbers
    or Sheets.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Lossy.</strong> Metadata and nested workout details do not fit a grid, and are not
    included. If you need them, export NDJSON or SQLite as well — nothing stops you doing both.
  </p>

  <h3>Markdown — for Obsidian and journals</h3>

  <p>
    One <code>YYYY-MM-DD.md</code> note per day, each carrying YAML front matter that Dataview can
    query, then the day in prose and small tables.
  </p>

  <Code caption="Front matter on a daily note" code={frontMatter} />

  <p>
    <strong>Lossy, and deliberately the opposite trade to the others.</strong> A note keeps a day's
    counts, totals and extremes and throws the records themselves away, along with metadata,
    sources, devices and sample identifiers. The picker says so, a <code>README.md</code> in the
    archive says so, and the foot of every single note says so and points at the formats that keep
    everything.
  </p>

  <p>
    Days are local days, and sleep is filed under the day it <em>ended</em>, because last night's
    sleep belongs to the morning you woke up.
  </p>

  <h3>GPX — for maps</h3>

  <p>
    One GPX 1.1 track per workout that has GPS. This is the odd one out, and the interface says so
    before you choose it: a GPX file is a track, with nowhere to put a heart rate or a body weight,
    so <strong>a GPX export contains only workouts and their routes and none of the rest of your
      Health data</strong>. A workout recorded without GPS produces no file — a treadmill run has no
    route, and that is not an error.
  </p>

  <p>
    Because a GPX run drains only workouts and workout routes, it avoids spending hours reading
    types that could not appear in the output.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="note" title="Gaps in a route are shown as gaps">
    <p>
      Routes arrive as pages at fixed absolute offsets, so an interrupted sync leaves a hole of a
      known size rather than a shorter list. Joining the two sides of a missing page would draw a
      straight line across a mile of city and look correct — so instead a gap becomes a separate{' '}
      <code>&lt;trkseg&gt;</code> on each side, the track's <code>&lt;desc&gt;</code> says how many
      points are missing and where, and the archive's <code>README.md</code> lists every affected
      file.
    </p>
    <p>
      Speed, course and Core Location's accuracies have no element in base GPX, and are published
      under Hozz's own namespace in <code>&lt;extensions&gt;</code>.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Delivery formats</h2>

  <p>
    An automatic destination receives one of five. These are a different list from the one above,
    and <a href="/docs/delivery-schema/">the delivery schema</a> documents each of them field by
    field with a worked example.
  </p>

  <Table
    caption="Delivery formats and what each keeps"
    columns={['Format', 'Media type', 'Notes']}
  >
    {
      DELIVERY_FORMATS.map((format) => (
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">
            {format.name}
            {!format.lossless && <> — lossy</>}
          </th>
          <td>
            <code>{format.contentType}</code>
          </td>
          <td>{format.note}</td>
        </tr>
      ))
    }
  </Table>

  <h3>Why SQLite and Markdown are not offered here</h3>

  <p>
    A delivery is an append of new records to an endpoint or a folder. A database file is not
    appendable over HTTP, and a day's note rewritten from one batch would replace a full day with a
    fraction of it. Offering either would produce something that looked like it was working and was
    quietly wrong, so neither is offered.
  </p>

  <h3>Why line protocol is not offered to folders</h3>

  <p>
    Hozz's own Mac app watches a folder for <code>ndjson</code>, <code>json</code> and{' '}
    <code>csv</code>, and ignores anything else. A folder destination writing line protocol would
    look like it was working while nothing was ever ingested, so that combination is not offered at
    all.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
